  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_1",
      "name": "Kear-Wilsdorf lock formation",
      "category": "dislocation locking",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "single crystal with γ-γ′ microstructure",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 2,
          "section": "Introduction",
          "quote": "In this temperature range, most of the dislocations in the intermetallic γ′ phase become immobile screw dislocations that are locked in a Kear–Wilsdorf (or KW) configuration due to cross-slip [7].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "Figure 2",
          "chunk_id": "7e3f4c662b6dc103:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "anti-phase boundary shearing",
      "category": "precipitate shearing",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "single crystal with γ-γ′ microstructure",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 4,
          "section": "2. Sub-Grain Scale for the γ-γ′ Microstructural of Nickel Superalloys",
          "quote": "Section 2 of this paper introduces the sub-grain scale dislocation density crystal plasticity constitutive laws with anti-phase boundary (APB) shearing of γ′ precipitates.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "7e3f4c662b6dc103:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "cubic slip",
      "category": "dislocation slip",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "single crystal",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "10",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "It can be observed that the yield stress at high temperature is much less than at room temperature due to the activation of cubic slip systems.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "Figure 4b",
          "chunk_id": "7e3f4c662b6dc103:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_4",
      "name": "geometrically-necessary dislocation hardening",
      "category": "strain hardening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "single crystal",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "18",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "Dislocations have a difficult time passing through the channel with less distance between precipitates, so they start to bow around the precipitates and result in the generation of another source of hardening, which is geometrically-necessary dislocations (GNDs). The generation of these dislocations accordingly results in increasing the yield stress.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "7e3f4c662b6dc103:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_5",
      "name": "Hall-Petch effect",
      "category": "grain size strengthening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "single crystal",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "18",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "According to this equation, the size of precipitates has the major effect on the yield stress, where the behavior shows a sort of Hall–Petch effect.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "7e3f4c662b6dc103:part:002"
        }
      ]
    }
